subtopics.theory_md:
Every sketch has two functions:
- **`setup()`** runs **once** at power-on or reset: set pin modes, start serial.
- **`loop()`** runs **repeatedly** forever.

Pin functions: `pinMode(pin, INPUT/OUTPUT/INPUT_PULLUP)`, `digitalWrite(pin, HIGH/LOW)`, `digitalRead(pin)`, `delay(ms)`. HIGH = 5 V, LOW = 0 V.

**Blink:**
```
void setup(){ pinMode(13, OUTPUT); }
void loop(){
  digitalWrite(13, HIGH); delay(1000);
  digitalWrite(13, LOW);  delay(1000);
}
```
An external LED needs a **series resistor**: $R=(V_{supply}-V_{LED})/I$.

questions.model_answer_md:
```
void setup() {
  pinMode(13, OUTPUT);   // pin 13 as output
}
void loop() {
  digitalWrite(13, HIGH); // LED on
  delay(1000);            // 1000 ms = 1 s
  digitalWrite(13, LOW);  // LED off
  delay(1000);
}
```
- **setup()** executes once at start/reset; used for pinMode and serial begin.
- **loop()** executes again and again as long as power is on.
- Pin 13 has the on-board LED, so no external part is needed.

```
void setup() {
  pinMode(2, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(13, OUTPUT);
}
void loop() {
  if (digitalRead(2) == LOW)  // pressed pulls pin to GND
    digitalWrite(13, HIGH);
  else
    digitalWrite(13, LOW);
}
```
**INPUT_PULLUP** enables an internal resistor to 5 V, so the pin reads HIGH when open and never floats; pressing connects it to GND, giving LOW.

```
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(9, OUTPUT);
}
void loop() {
  int v = analogRead(A0);          // 0-1023
  Serial.println(v);               // show on monitor
  int pwm = 255 - v / 4;           // scale 0-1023 to 0-255, inverted
  analogWrite(9, pwm);
  delay(500);
}
```
- `v/4` scales the 10-bit reading to the 8-bit PWM range.
- Pin 9 supports PWM.
- Serial Monitor must be set to 9600 baud.
